{"name":"Dinosaur Bone","hex":"#827563","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dinosaur","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Those animals of the clade Dinosauria that existed during the Triassic, Jurassic and Cretaceous periods and are now extinct. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Any of the animals belonging to the clade Dinosauria, including its sole surviving lineage: Neornithes."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Very old or old-fashioned."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dinosaur"},{"term":"bone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A composite material consisting largely of calcium phosphate and collagen and making up the skeleton of most vertebrates.","Any of the components of an endoskeleton, made of this material."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of an off-white colour, like the colour of bone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fossil","blocks":[{"text":"A fossil is any preserved remains, impression, or trace of any once-living thing from a past geological age. Examples include bones, shells, exoskeletons, stone imprints of animals or microbes, objects preserved in amber, hair, petrified wood and DNA remnants. The totality of fossils is known as the fossil record. Though the fossil record is incomplete, numerous studies have demonstrated that there is enough information available to give a good understanding of the pattern of diversification of life on Earth, and it can fill gaps in our understanding of the sequence of evolution on earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Fossil_Diversity.png/330px-Fossil_Diversity.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fossil","wikidata":"Q40614"},{"title":"Dinosaur","blocks":[{"text":"Dinosaurs are a diverse group of warmblooded reptiles of the clade Dinosauria. They existed through most of the Mesozoic era, first appearing early in the Triassic period. They became the dominant terrestrial vertebrates after the Triassic–Jurassic extinction event 201.3 mya and their dominance continued throughout the Jurassic and Cretaceous periods. The fossil record shows that birds are feathered dinosaurs, having evolved from earlier theropods during the Late Jurassic epoch, and are the only dinosaur lineage known to have survived the Cretaceous–Paleogene extinction event approximately 66 mya. Dinosaurs can therefore be divided into avian dinosaurs—birds—and the extinct non-avian dinosaurs, which are all dinosaurs other than birds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Sinosauropteryxfossil.jpg/330px-Sinosauropteryxfossil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dinosaur","wikidata":"Q430"},{"title":"Bone","blocks":[{"text":"A bone is a rigid organ that constitutes part of the skeleton in most vertebrate animals. Bones protect the organs of the body, produce red and white blood cells, store minerals, help regulate acid-base homeostasis, provide structure and support for the body, and enable mobility and hearing. Bones come in a variety of shapes and sizes and have complex internal and external structures."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Left_femur_of_extinct_elephant%2C_Alaska%2C_Ice_Age_Wellcome_L0057714.jpg/330px-Left_femur_of_extinct_elephant%2C_Alaska%2C_Ice_Age_Wellcome_L0057714.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bone","wikidata":"Q265868"},{"title":"Shades of white","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of white are colors that differ slightly from the CIE standard illuminant D65, a white point that represents the average color of daylight at noon. There isn't one objectively pure white, as noon daylight varies by location and atmospheric conditions, and the choice of using D65 instead of direct sunlight or a white point on the Planckian locus is arbitrary. For simplicity, this article will use the term pure white for the D65 white point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Color_icon_white.svg/330px-Color_icon_white.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_white","wikidata":"Q7460350"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Tyrannosaurus_Rex_Holotype.jpg/500px-Tyrannosaurus_Rex_Holotype.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Tyrannosaurus_Rex_Holotype.jpg/1280px-Tyrannosaurus_Rex_Holotype.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Tyrannosaurus_Rex_Holotype.jpg/500px-Tyrannosaurus_Rex_Holotype.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATyrannosaurus_Rex_Holotype.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Hyperborean-gryphon-persepolis-protoceratops-psittacosaurus-skeletons.jpg/500px-Hyperborean-gryphon-persepolis-protoceratops-psittacosaurus-skeletons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Hyperborean-gryphon-persepolis-protoceratops-psittacosaurus-skeletons.jpg/1280px-Hyperborean-gryphon-persepolis-protoceratops-psittacosaurus-skeletons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Hyperborean-gryphon-persepolis-protoceratops-psittacosaurus-skeletons.jpg/500px-Hyperborean-gryphon-persepolis-protoceratops-psittacosaurus-skeletons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser